  The "De-identification" Trap: 2025 research from USC explicitly confirmed the anonymization behavior you noted. They found that many doctors using ChatGPT were unknowingly committing HIPAA violations by pasting what they believed was "anonymized" patient data. Under the HIPAA Safe Harbor rule, 18 specific identifiers must be removed for data to be legally de-identified. Researchers found clinicians were routinely relying on quick redactions (like just removing names) and failing to strip all 18 identifiers before prompting the AI.  
  The Penalty Reality: When an employee pastes an improperly redacted patient chart into a consumer AI tool, it triggers the same legal consequences as a standard data breach. This requires the hospital to notify affected patients within 60 days, report the incident to the HHS Office for Civil Rights, and face fines ranging up to $1.5 million per violation category.  
